      "answer": "TL;DR: There\u2019s no direct evidence about Thalia\u2019s trustworthiness, but the situation strongly suggests caution\u2014something could be off.\n\nBased on the projection, **there are no explicit facts about Thalia\u2019s motives, behavior, or allegiance**, so you don\u2019t have enough grounded evidence to confirm trust or suspicion.\n\nHowever, two OBSERVED signals raise red flags about the *context* of the meeting:\n- The party received a clear warning: **\u201ctrust no one.\u201d**\n- **Stafl attacks Thalia after being revived**, which suggests either Thalia was perceived as a threat or something about her triggered that reaction.\n\nThese don\u2019t prove Thalia is compromised\u2014but they do indicate instability, paranoia, or hidden factors in play during the meeting.\n\nKey Attributes:\n- Evidence about Thalia: None in projection (insufficient data)\n- Contextual warning: \u201ctrust no one\u201d (OBSERVED)\n- Related incident: Stafl attacks Thalia after being revived (OBSERVED)\n- Party mental state: Disoriented and under pressure (CANON/OBSERVED)\n\nBottom line: You can\u2019t confirm she\u2019s untrustworthy\u2014but the scene is primed for deception, so trusting her outright would be risky.",
      "embedding_similarity": null
